THE PAYLOAD, FROM messages.jsonl
---------------------------------
Message 177's `old_string`, as stored:

    '/**\\n * Create starfield background effect\\n */\\n function createStar...'

Two faults in one string.

1. LITERAL BACKSLASH-N. `"\n" in old_string` is False; `"\\n" in old_string`
   is True. The whole 729-character payload is ONE LINE. `replace_symbol`
   failed on the same thing, and its error even described the consequence
   exactly: "2 unclosed { - the innermost was opened on line 133 and the file
   ends without closing it" - because a single line of JS with escaped
   newlines does not balance.

2. THE READ GUTTER. `read_file` numbers its output and the gutter ends in a
   space:

       74| function createStarField() {
       75|     const geometry = new THREE.BufferGeometry();

   Strip `74|` and you keep the separator. Line by line against the file, after
   decoding the escapes:

       4 of 22 lines exact
      18 of 22 lines exactly ONE SPACE too deep

   And the 4 exact ones are the JSDoc header ABOVE the read's first line, which
   the model typed from memory instead of copying. Every line it copied carried
   the space. That is not a coincidence, it is the mechanism.


WHY THE EXISTING REPAIRS DID NOT FIRE
--------------------------------------
Both were already there. Neither could reach it.

`_strip_line_numbers` handles the model pasting `74| ` back verbatim - and
correctly requires EVERY non-empty line to carry a gutter, or a markdown table
would get mangled. Here the model had already stripped `74|` itself, so no line
carried one. Right contract, wrong failure.

`_decode_literal_escapes` was called, and produced the correct text. Then:

    decoded = _decode_literal_escapes(old_string)
    if decoded != old_string and content.count(decoded) > 0:
        old_string = decoded            # <- ONLY if it matches EXACTLY

The decode was tested for an exact match, missed by the gutter space, and was
DISCARDED. Every line-based repair below then ran on the escaped one-liner,
which has no lines to split. The one usable version of the payload was computed
and thrown away, and the model was told "not found" about text it had sent
correctly apart from the escaping.

`_fuzzy_match_block` tolerates trailing whitespace only, and says why:
"leading indentation is compared exactly, so we never silently re-indent code."
That is the right default and it is what cost this edit.


THE FIX
-------
a) Every repair now runs on the text AS SENT and then on its DECODED form.
   The decode is no longer required to be exactly right to be worth trying.

b) `_indent_shifted_match_block`: match on stripped lines, require the match to
   be UNIQUE, require at least MIN_INDENT_FUZZ_LINES (3) lines, and return the
   FILE's own bytes. Three lines minimum because indentation is the only thing
   separating two identically-worded lines in different scopes, and matching
   one or two on stripped text alone is loose enough to land in the wrong
   place.

c) `_realign_replacement`: put `new_string` at the file's indentation PER LINE.
   Matching the block is half the job - writing the model's own text back at
   the model's own wrong indentation fixes the match and mis-indents the file,
   which in Python is not cosmetic.

   Per line, not per block, and this took two attempts to get right:

     - A common-prefix dedent does nothing here. The block's shared indent is
       "" both sides, because `/**` sits at column 0 in both.
     - A single MODAL shift is decided by whichever group is larger, which is
       not a property of the edit. It worked on the real payload (18 shifted vs
       4 correct) and produced the wrong answer on a 5-line block (3 correct vs
       2 shifted), silently dedenting the lines that were already right.

   So: each replacement line that also appears in `old_string` moves by exactly
   that line's own measured error, and a line that does not appear - one the
   model changed or added - inherits the last known correction above it. A
   rewritten line sits in the same block as its neighbours.


